  "semantic_claims": [
    {
      "claim_id": "majority-ground",
      "proposition": "The majority holds that 38 U.S.C. 511(a)'s 'questions of law and fact necessary to a decision by the Secretary under a law that affects the provision of benefits' takes the benefits statute as given, so a challenge to the constitutional validity of the statute itself is not such a question; it reads the VJRA as carrying forward Johnson v. Robison's construction of the 'decision under a law' phrase against the 1988 backdrop that agency tribunals did not decide the constitutionality of Acts of Congress, and rejects the government's argument that 7292(c)'s grant of Federal Circuit review of statutory validity implies exclusive channeling."
    },
    {
      "claim_id": "ground-breadth",
      "proposition": "The holding is confined to challenges attacking the validity of a benefits statute itself (its inherent terms); the opinion expressly leaves claims attacking VA action, including constitutional challenges to the VA's interpretation or application of a statute, channeled through the VJRA, and declines to reach petitioner's Article II/Appointments Clause argument about BVA authority."
    }
  ],
